But what about this? This symbol, the Jolly Roger, was once one of the most feared symbols in the world. It represented death, pirates, and poison. But today, it's associated more with treasure, lockbuster movies, or Halloween, than actual danger. We are surrounded by icons that warn us what to stay away from, what not to do, what to be afraid of. But how do you design a symbol in a way that will last across generations and languages?
那这个呢？这个符号，骷髅旗，曾经是世界上最令人恐惧的符号之一。它代表着死亡、海盗和毒药。但今天，它与宝藏、大片或万圣节的联系，比与实际危险的联系更多。我们周围到处都是警告我们远离、不要做什么、要害怕什么的图标。但是，你该如何设计一个能跨越世代和语言的符号呢？

3
00:00:52.000 --> 00:01:22.000
It turns out that is an incredibly hard thing to do. Back in the early 20th century, there was an urgent need for a new kind of warning symbol. At the time, there was no universal standard for communicating the presence of dangerous biological materials. Laboratories at the US Army used an inverted blue triangle, those at the Navy used a pink rectangle. The Universal Postal Convention used a white staff and snake on a violet background. There was no consistency in the visual language used to communicate risk.
事实证明，这极其困难。20世纪初，人们迫切需要一种新型的警告符号。当时，对于如何传达危险生物材料的存在，并没有统一的标准。美国陆军的实验室使用倒置的蓝色三角形，海军的实验室使用粉色矩形。万国邮政联盟在紫色背景上使用白色的蛇杖。在传达风险的视觉语言上，没有任何一致性。

4
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:57.000
That was dangerous and could lead to accidental infections. So in 1966, a group of engineers and designers at Dow Chemical set out to create the best possible icon for biohazardous materials. They laid out six design criteria. First it needed to be visually striking so that it would draw immediate attention. That ruled out simple shapes like those from the Navy and Army. It also had to be unique and unambiguous in order not to be confused with symbols used for other purposes. That ruled out the snake and staff which has multiple versions and has a pretty vague meaning as a general symbol
这很危险，可能导致意外感染。因此，1966年，陶氏化学的一组工程师和设计师着手为生物危害材料创造最佳图标。他们提出了六项设计标准。首先，它需要视觉上引人注目，以便立即引起注意。这排除了海军和陆军使用的简单形状。它还必须独特且明确，以免与用于其他目的的符号混淆。这排除了蛇杖，因为它有多个版本，作为通用符号含义相当模糊。

5
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:25.000
for medicine. On top of that it had to be quickly recognizable and easily recalled. It had to be easy to stencil and rotationally symmetrical in order to appear identical from all angles. And lastly, it had to be acceptable to groups of all backgrounds. So the Dow Chemical team designed an experiment. Charles Baldwin, an environmental health engineer behind the experiment, said that the team wanted something that was memorable but meaningless, so we could educate people as to what it means.
对于医学来说。最重要的是，它必须能被快速识别并易于记忆。它必须易于模版印刷，并且具有旋转对称性，以便从各个角度看起来都一样。最后，它必须被各种背景的群体所接受。因此，陶氏化学团队设计了一个实验。该实验背后的环境健康工程师查尔斯·鲍德温表示，团队想要一些令人难忘但没有意义的东西，这样我们就可以教育人们它意味着什么。

6
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:46.000
They showed a set of 24 symbols to 300 people from 25 American cities. There were six newly designed biohazard markers and 18 common symbols, things like Mr. Pina, the Texaco Star, the Shell Oil symbol, the Red Cross, and a Swastika. Participants were asked to guess the meaning of each one, which was used to assign each one a meaningfulness score.
他们向来自美国25个城市的300人展示了一组24个符号。其中包括六个新设计的生物危害标记和18个常见符号，比如皮纳先生、德士古之星、壳牌石油符号、红十字会和纳粹万字旗。参与者被要求猜测每个符号的含义，这被用来为每个符号分配一个意义得分。

