A week later, the same participants were shown those original 24 symbols plus 36 more. They were asked to identify which symbols they remembered seeing in the previous round of the study. Among the six competing biohazard designs, this one stood out. It scored the highest in memorability, but the lowest in meaningfulness, so it was unforgettable, but also a totally blank slate for designers who wanted to give it meaning. And with that, it became a national standard. It's easy to overlook how much visual communication work these symbols are doing.
一周后，同样的参与者被展示了最初的24个符号加上另外36个符号。他们被要求识别出他们在研究的前一轮中记得见过的符号。在六个竞争的生物危害设计中，这一个脱颖而出。它在可记忆性方面得分最高，但在意义方面得分最低，所以它令人难忘，但对于想要赋予它意义的设计师来说，它也是一张完全的白纸。就这样，它成为了国家标准。人们很容易忽视这些符号在视觉传达方面所做的工作。

